<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Self-Filtered Distillation with LLMs-generated Trust Indicators 
<br class="ltx_break">for Reliable Patent Classification</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yongmin Yoo<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xu Zhang<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Longbing Cao
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Frontier AI Research Centre, Macquarie University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Computing, FSE, Macquarie University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yooyongmin91@gmail.com</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{xu.zhang12</span>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">longbing.cao}@mq.edu.au</span>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><sup id="id6" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>These authors contributed equally to this work.
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) increasingly generate natural language rationales to enhance interpretability, but these often contain logical errors, label mismatches, and domain-specific misalignments. Directly using such rationales as supervision risks propagating noise and undermining training stability. To address this challenge, we introduce Self-Filtered Distillation, a framework specifically tailored for patent classification, which treats LLM-generated rationales as trust signals rather than ground-truth supervision. The framework employs selective distillation guided by three unsupervised trust metrics: (1) Self-Consistency, which measures the stability of LLM-generated rationales across multiple generations; (2) Class Entailment Alignment, which assesses semantic coherence with patent-specific class definitions; and (3) LLM Agreement Scoring, which validates rationale–label plausibility. These metrics are integrated into a unified trust score that primarily weights training samples while optionally filtering out extremely low-trust cases, enabling reasoning-aware supervision. Experiments on the USPTO-2M dataset, a widely used benchmark for patent classification, show that our method outperforms label-based learning and conventional distillation in accuracy, stability, and interpretability, establishing a reliable paradigm for leveraging reasoning-aware trust indicators in patent analytics.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Patent classification is critical for organizing large-scale patent corpora into technical classification schemes, serving as the foundation for applications such as intellectual property management, technology scouting, and innovation analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang and Goetz, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Unlike general-domain texts, patents exhibit a multifaceted nature: highly specialized terminology, complex claim structures, and strategically ambiguous expressions, which pose significant challenges for automated classification. Our approach is specifically tailored to the patent domain, distinguishing it from general text classification techniques. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) offer strong potential to address these challenges by processing massive patent datasets with improved accuracy and generating natural language rationales that enhance interpretability and provide a reasoning-based foundation for trust in classification outcomes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee and Hsiang, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05431v1/figure/intro_distillation.jpg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/186;" width="471" height="186" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Direct distillation vs. self-filtered distillation: Weighting and filtering rationales through trust metrics yields more stable and interpretable student models.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond predictive accuracy, research in NLP has increasingly emphasized explanation-driven learning as a means of enhancing interpretability and trustworthiness. This line of work leverages human-annotated rationales as auxiliary supervision signals, demonstrating that explanation can improve both generalization and interpretability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Camburu et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeYoung et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. While promising, such approaches incur high annotation costs and are often limited to short texts and simple classification tasks. Consequently, they fall short when applied to the classification of multifaceted and lengthy patents, where explanation-driven supervision faces domain-specific challenges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With the advent of LLMs, research has increasingly shifted toward leveraging model-generated rationales to strengthen learning without requiring expensive human annotation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsieh et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. While this paradigm offers scalability and interpretability, LLM-generated rationales often contain logical inconsistencies, label mismatches, and semantic misalignment with domain-specific classification schemes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turpin et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, unsuitable for multifaceted patents. For instance, an LLM may justify categorizing a digital communication patent (H04L) based on antenna structures, or misclassify an artificial intelligence patent (G06N) as general data processing (G06F). Such errors extend beyond performance degradation: in the patent domain, they can lead to misguided technology mapping, flawed market analysis, or even intellectual property disputes. Treating all rationales as direct supervisory signals risks propagating these errors and reducing training stability. Therefore, high-stakes applications of patent classification demand a framework that can quantitatively assess rationale quality and selectively incorporate only trustworthy rationales <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lampinen et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address this challenge, <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>elf-<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>iltered <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>istillation (SFD) reinterprets LLM-generated rationales as trust indicators rather than ground-truth labels, dynamically weighting their contribution and filtering out extremely low-trust cases. We propose three unsupervised trust metrics to guide the distillation process: (1) Self-Consistency, which measures the stability of multiple rationales generated for the same input; (2) Class Entailment Alignment, which evaluates semantic coherence between rationales and class-specific definitions; and (3) LLM Agreement Scoring, which employs an external verifier LLM to assess the plausibility of rationale-label pairs. These metrics are aggregated into a unified trust score that dynamically modulates the contribution of each training instance during distillation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Empirical evaluations on the USPTO-2M dataset demonstrate that SFD surpasses traditional label-based learning and conventional distillation methods, achieving superior performance in predictive accuracy, training stability, and interpretability. This indicates that trust-based LLM rationale modulation not only enhances classification performance but also establishes a new learning paradigm for effectively leveraging LLM-generated rationales.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The primary contributions include:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Trust-based distillation paradigm</span>: which treats LLM-generated rationales as trust indicators rather than ground-truth signals, reducing instability and error propagation while turning model explanations into reliable learning resources.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unsupervised trust metrics</span>: consist of Self-Consistency, Class Entailment Alignment, and LLM Agreement Scoring, which evaluate rationale quality without human annotations and provide a scalable mechanism for weighting and filtering noisy rationales.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Patent-domain validation</span>: Applied to patent classification, the SFD outperforms label-based learning and conventional distillation in accuracy, stability, and interpretability, demonstrating its value for trustworthy patent analytics.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Patent Classification</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Patent classification is a central task in intellectual property management, supporting prior art search, and legal decision-making. Early studies used TF–IDF features with classifiers such as SVMs or logistic regression, with limited performance due to domain-specific terminology and high-dimensional feature spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fall et al., 2003</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Deep learning brings about significant improvements: CNN- and RNN-based models capture semantic and structural patterns from claims and abstracts, outperforming methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. Still, the challenges of long documents, technical vocabulary, and concept interdependencies make patent classification harder than general text classification. Transformer-based models open a new paradigm. Domain-adapted encoders such as PatentBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee and Hsiang, 2019</a>)</cite> and PatentSBERTa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bekamiri et al., 2021</a>)</cite> outperform BERTs, while generative approaches like PatentGPT show the potential of large-scale pretraining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Leveraging the hierarchical structure of the Cooperative Patent Classification (CPC) also helps address the complexity of multi-label classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Large-scale datasets like USPTO-2M, with millions of patents and thousands of CPC classes, further accelerate progress but also introduce issues of computational cost and severe label imbalance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Risch and Krestel, 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, research has advanced from statistical models to transformer-based approaches, achieving steady gains. Yet the patent nature, including a hierarchical CPC system, long documents, domain-specific language, and label imbalance, has open challenges. For high-stakes patent applications, accuracy must be complemented by transparency and interpretability, motivating more reliable, explanation-aware frameworks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Explanation-driven Learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting enables LLMs to solve complex reasoning tasks by generating intermediate rationales, improving predictive performance and interpretability across arithmetic reasoning, commonsense inference, and logical deduction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. This has inspired the distillation research that reasoning traces from large teacher models are transferred into smaller students <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsieh et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. For instance, KeyPoint-CoT compresses lengthy rationales into concise key points for efficiency and faithfulness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Yet most distillation approaches assume uniform rationale quality, which risks propagating inconsistent or erroneous explanations. This is especially problematic in patent classification, where misaligned rationales can distort label assignments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Explanation-driven learning more broadly uses rationales as auxiliary supervision. Datasets like e-SNLI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Camburu et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and ERASER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeYoung et al., 2020</a>)</cite> show that human-written explanations improve generalization and transparency, while in-context rationales aid robustness in few-shot learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lampinen et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wiegreffe et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. However, LLM-generated rationales often suffer from incompleteness, unfaithfulness, or contradictions with labels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turpin et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. These issues are amplified in patent classification due to multifaceted patent features including lengthy claims, technical vocabulary, and complex categorization schemes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, while CoT prompting and explanation-driven learning highlight the value of LLM rationales, their unstable quality poses risks if applied directly. Our framework addresses this by treating rationales not as deterministic supervision but as trust indicators that dynamically adjust each instance’s training weight. This preserves interpretability and generalization benefits while ensuring stability and reliability in high-stakes domains such as patent classification.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Trustworthiness and Selective Learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ensuring the trustworthiness of LLM predictions has become a critical issue as LLMs are increasingly used in decision-sensitive contexts. A major line of work improves reliability at the inference time. Self-Consistency aggregates multiple reasoning paths for stable outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, selective prediction methods allow abstention under uncertainty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoshikawa and Okazaki, 2023</a>)</cite>, and self-evaluation frameworks train models to assign confidence scores <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Uncertainty-aware fine-tuning further mitigates hallucinations and stabilizes reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krishnan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. While introducing effective safeguards, these methods remain largely confined to inference-level mechanisms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our work instead integrates trust indicators into the training process. Rather than filtering outputs post hoc, we use rationale quality to dynamically reweight training instances. Specifically, we extend Self-Consistency as a measure of rationale stability during learning, and complement it with Class Entailment Alignment and LLM Agreement Scoring to evaluate semantic coherence and external plausibility. This selective distillation strategy makes the training procedure itself reliability-aware.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Such integration is important in domains like patent classification, where low-quality rationales can propagate errors with legal and economic impact. By embedding trust indicators into distillation, our framework advances beyond inference-level reliability toward responsible training practices, ensuring model improvements are guided by explanations that are both stable and faithful.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05431v1/figure/method_distill.jpg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/287;" width="471" height="287" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overview of the proposed Self-Filtered Distillation(SFD) framework: It consists of three stages. STEP 1: LLM-based prediction and rationale generation, where both labels and natural language rationales are produced. STEP 2: Reasoning quality evaluation through three unsupervised trust indicators: Self-Consistency (SC), Class Entailment Alignment (CEA), and LLM Agreement Score (LAS). STEP 3: Trust-aware training control, which modulates the loss contribution using soft weighting and threshold-based filtering to suppress low-quality rationales and enhance reliable signals.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Self-Filtered Distillation (SFD) framework, shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.3 Trustworthiness and Selective Learning ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Self-Filtered Distillation with LLMs-generated Trust Indicators for Reliable Patent Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, quantitatively evaluates the quality of reasoning generated by LLMs and dynamically adjusts the learning contribution according to its estimated trustworthiness. The training process consists of three stages: Step 1 with LLM-based prediction and reasoning generation, Step 2 with reasoning quality evaluation, and Step 3 for trust-aware loss modulation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>LLM-Based Prediction and Reasoning Generation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our setting, an LLM receives an input text <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and is prompted to generate two outputs: (1) a predicted label or a probability distribution over labels, and (2) a natural language rationale <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> that explains the prediction. While the predicted label is used for training, the generated rationale is not treated as a ground-truth supervision signal. Instead, it serves as auxiliary information for estimating the reliability of the sample.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of candidate labels. The model outputs a probability vector <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{y}}\in[0,1]^{|\mathcal{Y}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{y}}\in[0,1]^{|\mathcal{Y}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each component <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the confidence for label <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. The true label is denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. The model is trained to minimize a label-based loss using <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. At the inference time, thresholds or task-specific decision rules are applied to derive final predictions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05431v1/figure/three_method.jpg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/260;" width="471" height="260" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Overview of the three trust indicators in our Reasoning Quality Evaluation: SC ensures stability, CEA verifies semantic alignment, and LAS provides external validation.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Reasoning Quality Evaluation: Lightweight Trust Scoring</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Rather than serving as ground-truth supervision, LLM-generated reasoning is interpreted as a trust signal that reflects whether the reasoning is reliable enough to guide training. This enables a selective distillation mechanism: high-trust rationales contribute more to model updates, while noisy or inconsistent rationales are automatically downweighted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the trustworthiness of rationales without relying on human annotations, we introduce three complementary unsupervised metrics, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 LLM-Based Prediction and Reasoning Generation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Self-Filtered Distillation with LLMs-generated Trust Indicators for Reliable Patent Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Each captures a distinct facet of rationale quality.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(1) Self-Consistency Scoring (SC)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the logical stability of LLM-generated reasoning, we prompt the same model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times with identical input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, obtaining a set of reasonings <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r_{1},\dots,r_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r_{1},\dots,r_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The prompt used for this multi-sample generation is detailed in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Prompt for Self-Consistency (SC) ‣ Self-Filtered Distillation with LLMs-generated Trust Indicators for Reliable Patent Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. We then compute the average pairwise cosine similarity between their sentence embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SC}(x)=\frac{2}{k(k-1)}\sum_{i&lt;j}\cos\left(\phi(r_{i}),\phi(r_{j})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>SC</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SC}(x)=\frac{2}{k(k-1)}\sum_{i&lt;j}\cos\left(\phi(r_{i}),\phi(r_{j})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Higher SC values indicate that the model produces consistent rationales across runs, suggesting stable reasoning with reduced stochasticity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For instance, in the context of patent classification, when the same document concerning image classification produces divergent rationales across multiple generations, such as one emphasizing “machine learning techniques” and another focusing on “neural network architectures,” the resulting SC score is low. Such inconsistency indicates a lack of reasoning stability, which our framework interprets as reduced reliability during training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(2) Class Entailment Alignment (CEA)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CEA measures how well the reasoning <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> semantically aligns with each predicted label <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. For every label, we generate the task ontology using an LLM to obtain its definition <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\hat{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\hat{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then compute the cosine similarity between the embeddings of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\hat{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\hat{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CEA}(x)=\frac{1}{|\hat{Y}|}\sum_{\hat{y}\in\hat{Y}}\cos\bigl(\phi(r),\phi(d_{\hat{y}})\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>CEA</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CEA}(x)=\frac{1}{|\hat{Y}|}\sum_{\hat{y}\in\hat{Y}}\cos\bigl(\phi(r),\phi(d_{\hat{y}})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By averaging over the predicted label set <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, CEA evaluates the degree to which the rationale is consistent with the label space. The Cooperative Patent Classification (CPC) system follows a hierarchical structure, where broader technical domains (e.g., G06F for computer technology) contain more specific subclasses (e.g., G06N for artificial intelligence). In CEA, we explicitly leverage this property by extracting the formal definitions of predicted CPC codes through an LLM and aligning them with the generated rationale. For multi-label cases, where a patent may belong to several subclasses simultaneously, CEA averages the alignment across all candidate CPC definitions, ensuring that the rationale remains coherent with the domain-specific taxonomy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For instance, in patent classification, when a document is assigned to H04L (digital communication) but the generated rationale emphasizes aspects of “antenna structure” rather than “signal transmission protocols,” the semantic mismatch results in a low CEA score. This reflects misalignment between the rationale and the formal CPC definition, which our framework treats as an indicator of reduced trustworthiness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(3) LLM Agreement Scoring (LAS)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LAS assesses the external plausibility of reasoning using an independent LLM-based verifier <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LLM}_{\mathrm{judge}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>LLM</mi><mi>judge</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LLM}_{\mathrm{judge}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is separate from the model that generates the reasoning. Generative models often exhibit overconfidence in their own outputs, which can lead to unreliable rationales if left unchecked. To mitigate this issue, the verifier receives a reasoning–label pair <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,\hat{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,\hat{y})</annotation></semantics></math> and outputs a scalar confidence score. The detailed judging prompt is provided in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Prompt for LLM Agreement Scoring (LAS) ‣ Self-Filtered Distillation with LLMs-generated Trust Indicators for Reliable Patent Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{LAS}(x)=\sigma\left(\mathrm{LLM}_{\mathrm{judge}}(r,\hat{y})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>LAS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>LLM</mi><mi>judge</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{LAS}(x)=\sigma\left(\mathrm{LLM}_{\mathrm{judge}}(r,\hat{y})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function mapping the raw score into <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. By introducing an external verifier, LAS provides an additional validation layer that counteracts model overconfidence and complements the other trust metrics by evaluating plausibility from an independent perspective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For instance, in cases where the original text concerns “data encryption methods” but the generated rationale incorrectly frames the invention as addressing “data compression,” the external verifier assigns a low LAS score. This penalization captures the lack of plausibility in the rationale–label alignment and highlights the necessity of incorporating independent validation to mitigate overconfidence in model-generated explanations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">SC, CEA, and LAS offer complementary perspectives on reasoning quality: SC captures the stability across multiple generations, CEA leverages the CPC taxonomy for semantic alignment, and LAS evaluates the external plausibility through independent validation. Unlike reference-based metrics such as BLEU or ROUGE, which rely on observable textual similarity, our trust indicators assess deeper aspects of reasoning that are crucial for patent classification. This design not only supports rigorous and trustworthy selective distillation but also enables the detection of unreliable rationales that traditional similarity metrics would overlook.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Trust-Aware Training Control</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Combined Trust Score (CTS) derived from our multi-aspect evaluation is used to modulate the learning contribution of each training sample. Our framework adopts a soft weighting strategy where all samples participate in training, but their loss contributions are scaled proportionally to their estimated trustworthiness. For instance, a sample with a low trust score, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CTS}(x)\approx 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>CTS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CTS}(x)\approx 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, has its influence significantly reduced. This approach mitigates the negative impact of low-quality reasoning without entirely discarding potentially informative signals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let the model <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> predict a probability vector <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{y}}=f_{\theta}(x)\in[0,1]^{|\mathcal{Y}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{y}}=f_{\theta}(x)\in[0,1]^{|\mathcal{Y}|}</annotation></semantics></math> for an input <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the ground-truth label denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. The task-specific loss function, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Loss}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Loss</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Loss}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., cross-entropy for classification), is scaled by the trust score <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CTS}(x)\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>CTS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CTS}(x)\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The final trust score, referred to as CTS, is computed as the direct average of the three previously defined metrics (SC, CEA, and LAS). This weighting scheme treats each dimension of trustworthiness as equally important, providing a balanced and robust measure of rationale quality without introducing additional learnable parameters. The CTS is thus defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CTS}(x)=\frac{1}{3}\big(\mathrm{SC}(x)+\mathrm{CEA}(x)+\mathrm{LAS}(x)\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>CTS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>SC</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>CEA</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>LAS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CTS}(x)=\frac{1}{3}\big(\mathrm{SC}(x)+\mathrm{CEA}(x)+\mathrm{LAS}(x)\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The overall training objective is to minimize the trust-weighted loss across all samples in data <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\sum_{x\in\mathcal{D}}\mathrm{CTS}(x)\cdot\mathrm{Loss}\big(f_{\theta}(x),\,y\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>CTS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>Loss</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\sum_{x\in\mathcal{D}}\mathrm{CTS}(x)\cdot\mathrm{Loss}\big(f_{\theta}(x),\,y\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to this soft weighting, we introduce a thresholding mechanism to completely exclude samples with extremely low trust scores. An indicator function, <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{1}\{\cdot\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{1}\{\cdot\}</annotation></semantics></math>, is applied to filter out any sample whose trust score falls below a predefined threshold <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\sum_{x\in\mathcal{D}}{1}\{\mathrm{CTS}(x)\geq\tau\}\cdot\mathrm{CTS}(x)\cdot\mathrm{Loss}\big(f_{\theta}(x),\,y\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>CTS</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>CTS</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>Loss</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\sum_{x\in\mathcal{D}}{1}\{\mathrm{CTS}(x)\geq\tau\}\cdot\mathrm{CTS}(x)\cdot\mathrm{Loss}\big(f_{\theta}(x),\,y\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Candidate thresholds, such as <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\{0.1,0.2,0.3,\ldots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\{0.1,0.2,0.3,\ldots\}</annotation></semantics></math>, are evaluated on a validation set, and the optimal value <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is selected for the final training. This strategy of combining soft weighting and hard thresholding ensures the model robust against noisy rationales while effectively leveraging high-quality reasoning signals.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments and Results</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the proposed framework by addressing four research questions that collectively assess its effectiveness, robustness, and interpretability in patent classification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1</span>: How does SFD compare with baseline methods?
<span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2</span>: Does threshold-based filtering improve robustness compared to soft weighting?
<span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3</span>: What is the effect of removing each trust metric?
<span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ4</span>: Are trust scores correlated with human judgments of rationale quality?</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Dataset</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we employ the USPTO-2M dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">United States Patent and Trademark Office, </a>)</cite>. USPTO-2M is a large-scale dataset released by the United States Patent and Trademark Office (USPTO), consisting of more than two million patent documents and thousands of CPC codes. This dataset has become one of the most widely used benchmarks for multi-label patent classification and has been consistently adopted as a reliable evaluation resource in numerous research efforts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee and Hsiang, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bekamiri et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Patent documents differ substantially from general-domain texts due to their use of specialized terminology, complex sentence structures, and strong interdependencies among technical concepts. These characteristics introduce significant challenges for automated classification. To ensure both coverage and feasibility, we restrict the label space to the subclass level of the CPC hierarchy. This setting prevents over-fragmentation of labels while maintaining sufficient representativeness of the data, as widely adopted in prior patent classification studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee and Hsiang, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haghighian Roudsari et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Risch et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, USPTO-2M serves as an appropriate evaluation basis for this work. It not only reflects the inherent challenges of patent data such as large scale, complexity, and hierarchical structure, but also provides a robust foundation for fair comparison across different modeling approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Qwen3-30B is used as the teacher model with Qwen3-0.6B as the student model in our distillation framework. For self-consistency, we generate three rationales per instance, balancing efficiency and stability. Additional rationales yield marginal gains with higher cost. Qwen3-32B serves as the Judge LLM for LAS computation, providing an independent evaluation of rationale–label plausibility. The trust coefficients <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\lambda_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\lambda_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed at <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/3</annotation></semantics></math>, treating each metric equally as in the definition of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CTS}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CTS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CTS}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. We optimize the student model using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 1e-5. All experiments are conducted on the USPTO-2M dataset, as described in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Dataset ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Self-Filtered Distillation with LLMs-generated Trust Indicators for Reliable Patent Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>RQ1. How does SFD compare with baseline methods?</h3>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:155.3pt;height:46.9pt;vertical-align:-22.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-63.4pt,19.2pt) scale(0.550406581252551,0.550406581252551) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F1-Micro</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F1-Macro</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Label-only Distillation</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.821</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.401</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Naive CoT Distillation</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.942</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.401</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Self-Consistency Distillation</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.932</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.354</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">LLM-as-a-Judge Distillation</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.893</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.355</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Rationale-Augmented Distillation</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.944</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.369</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SFD</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.960</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.404</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Performance comparison of SFD against baseline methods on USPTO-2M. Results show improvements in both Micro-F1 and Macro-F1, indicating enhanced effectiveness and robustness.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first examine whether the proposed SFD framework outperforms existing baseline approaches. Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.3 RQ1. How does SFD compare with baseline methods? ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Self-Filtered Distillation with LLMs-generated Trust Indicators for Reliable Patent Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the experimental results on the USPTO-2M dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The label-only distillation baseline shows the weakest performance with an F1-Micro of 0.821 and an F1-Macro of 0.401. Naive CoT distillation achieves a strong F1-Micro of 0.942, but its F1-Macro remains at 0.401, indicating limited improvement on minority classes. Self-consistency distillation and LLM-as-a-judge distillation record 0.932/0.354 and 0.893/0.355 respectively, showing partial gains but lacking balance across labels. Rationale-augmented distillation reaches 0.944/0.369, which is relatively stable yet still below the best result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, SFD achieves the top scores with 0.960 and 0.404 (F1-Micro, F1-Macro), surpassing all baselines. The improvement in Macro-F1 highlights that the method delivers reliable reasoning signals for rare categories. These findings demonstrate that treating LLM-generated rationales as trust indicators and selectively incorporating them into training yields both performance gains and improved robustness in patent classification.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>RQ2. Does threshold-based filtering improve robustness compared to soft weighting?</h3>

<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:310.5pt;height:96.5pt;vertical-align:-45.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(18.3pt,-5.7pt) scale(1.13329784164376,1.13329784164376) ;">
<table id="S4.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Threshold (<math id="S4.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F1-Micro</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F1-Macro</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subset Acc.</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">No Filtering</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.821</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.401</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.648</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0.5</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.820</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.400</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.646</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0.6</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.828</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.421</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.660</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0.7</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.837</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.439</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.677</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0.8</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.890</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.462</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.793</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0.9</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.960</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.404</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.7.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.930</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Results of threshold-based filtering on USPTO-2M, showing the trade-off between Micro-F1, Macro-F1, and Subset Accuracy across different <math id="S4.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> values.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare two strategies: (i) soft weighting, where all samples contribute proportionally to their trust scores (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0</annotation></semantics></math>); and (ii) threshold-based filtering, which excludes samples with <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CTS}(x)&lt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>CTS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CTS}(x)&lt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.4 RQ2. Does threshold-based filtering improve robustness compared to soft weighting? ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Self-Filtered Distillation with LLMs-generated Trust Indicators for Reliable Patent Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, mild filtering (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0.5</annotation></semantics></math>) yields limited improvement, while progressively stronger thresholds lead to more robust performance. At <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0.8</annotation></semantics></math>, the model achieves the highest Macro-F1 (0.462), showing strong reliability for rare classes. At <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, both Micro-F1 (0.960) and Subset Accuracy (0.930) reach their peak values, indicating that retaining only the most trustworthy rationales produces the most stable and reliable predictions overall. This demonstrates that high-threshold filtering effectively suppresses noisy reasoning while preserving informative signals. Consequently, we select <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0.9</annotation></semantics></math> as the optimal configuration for subsequent experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>RQ3. What is the effect of removing each trust metric?</h3>

<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:327.8pt;height:125.9pt;vertical-align:-59.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(37.4pt,-14.3pt) scale(1.29519830606129,1.29519830606129) ;">
<table id="S4.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
