<td id="S4.T4.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T4.2.8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.685</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T4.2.8.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T4.2.8.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Pearson correlation (<math id="S4.T4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>) between trust scores and human judgments on 1,000 samples of USPTO-2M.
<math id="S4.T4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and Rel. <math id="S4.T4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> denote absolute and relative differences from <math id="S4.T4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CTS}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CTS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CTS}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We sample 1,000 instances from USPTO-2M with varied trust scores. Three annotators rate each rationale on a 1–5 Likert scale for logical consistency, task alignment, and plausibility, with Krippendorff’s <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.641" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.641</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.641</annotation></semantics></math>. Guidelines are in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Annotation Guidelines ‣ Self-Filtered Distillation with LLMs-generated Trust Indicators for Reliable Patent Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.6 RQ4. Are trust scores correlated with human judgments of reasoning quality? ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Self-Filtered Distillation with LLMs-generated Trust Indicators for Reliable Patent Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that the <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CTS}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CTS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CTS}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> correlates strongest with human ratings, <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.685" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.685</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.685</annotation></semantics></math>. CEA is most critical, with its removal causing the largest drop at -30.66%, followed by LAS at -22.92%, and SC at -7.88%. <math id="S4.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CTS}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CTS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CTS}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> enhances the interpretability in patent classification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, these findings demonstrate that all three metrics contribute meaningfully, with CEA playing the most critical role. <math id="S4.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CTS}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CTS</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CTS}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> provides a more reliable and interpretable signal of reasoning quality, aligning closely with human judgments in complex domains such as patent classification.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper proposes SFD, a framework that improves reliability and accuracy in LLM-based patent classification by leveraging reasoning quality as a supervision signal. Unlike prior methods relying solely on model outputs, our approach evaluates rationales with trust metrics and incorporates them into training. Three trust metrics comprise Stability Consistency, Class Entailment Alignment, and Logic Agreement Score. Their aggregated scores dynamically weight or filter training samples, allowing the model to emphasize high-quality rationales while suppressing noisy ones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">SFD leads to threefold contributions: (1) establishing a protocol for assessing rationale trustworthiness, (2) designing a trust-driven weighting and filtering strategy, and (3) demonstrating consistent gains across LLM backbones and patent benchmarks. Experimental results show that trust-aware supervision not only enhances predictive performance and interpretability, but also establishes a new paradigm for reliable LLM-based reasoning in high-stakes patent analytics. As for future work, we will extend the SFD framework to other high-stakes domains such as biomedical and legal texts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the proposed Self-Filtered Distillation framework demonstrates consistent improvements over strong baselines, several considerations and avenues for future work remain. Our experiments are conducted on the USPTO-2M dataset, which consists of English-language patents and serves as the most widely used and reliable benchmark in patent NLP research. This choice ensures comparability and validity, yet relying solely on English patents limits the linguistic diversity of our evaluation. Future work could extend the framework to multilingual patent corpora, thereby enhancing its generality and applicability across different linguistic contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, the framework has been designed and validated within the patent domain. Patents exhibit typical characteristics of high-stakes text, including specialized terminology, complex structures, and strict accuracy requirements, which make them an appropriate testbed. Nevertheless, extending the framework to other high-stakes domains such as medicine, law, or scientific reporting would further demonstrate its robustness and position it as a more broadly applicable trust-aware learning paradigm.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethical Considerations</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study is conducted on the publicly available USPTO-2M dataset, and thus does not involve issues related to copyright or personal data. However, the proposed trust-based learning framework is designed for high-stakes domains such as patents, where misinterpretation of model-generated or model-evaluated reasoning could potentially influence legal or industrial decision-making. Therefore, the methods presented in this work are intended for research purposes, and any practical deployment should be accompanied by careful expert validation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While the framework employs reasonable trust indicators, including Self-Consistency, Class Entailment Alignment, and LLM Agreement Scoring, these measures cannot fully replace human judgment.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For transparency, we note that ChatGPT was used to improve grammar and clarity of expression.</p>
</div>
</section>
